     <bioghist encodinganalog="545$a">
      <head>Historical Information
</head>
<p><emph render="bold">Locality History:</emph> Mathews County was named for Thomas Mathews, of Norfolk, the Speaker of the Virginia House of Delegates when it was formed from Gloucester County by a statute passed on 16 December 1790 to take effect on 1 May 1791.</p>
    </bioghist>
    <scopecontent encodinganalog="520$a">
      <head>Scope and Content
</head>
      <p>Mathews County (Va.) Tax and Fiscal Records, 1792-1980 consist of assessments and reassessments of lands, capitation tax lists, Commonwealth claims, county appraisals, county bonds, county claims, delinquent lands, delinquent taxe lists, insolvent property and capitation taxes, land books, liquor licenses, liquor bonds, lists of real estate, oyster law taxes, personal property tax lists, reassessment requests, Sheriff's accounts, State Corporation Commission tax reports, surveys of Virginia land assessments, tables of tracts of land, tax exemptions, and Treasurer's records. 
</p>
    </scopecontent>
        <arrangement encodinganalog="351">
<head>Arrangement
</head>
<p>For descriptive purposes, the collection has been divided into two series:
<list type="simple"><item>Series I: Processed Tax and Fiscal Records, 1794, 1854-1903 </item>
<item>Series II: Unprocessed Tax and Fiscal Records, 1792-1980 </item>
</list>
</p>
</arrangement>
    <descgrp type="add">
      <head>Adjunct Descriptive Data
</head>
      <relatedmaterial encodinganalog="544$a">
        <head>Related Material
</head>
        <p>Additional Mathews County Tax and Fiscal Records can be found on microfilm at the Library of Virginia.  Consult <extref xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="https://www.lva.virginia.gov/public/local/results_all.asp?CountyID=VA169">"A Guide to Virginia County and City Records on Microfilm."</extref></p>
        <p>See the <extref xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:type="simple" xlink:href="http://www.virginiamemory.com/collections/lost">Lost Records Localities Digital Collection</extref> available at Virginia Memory.
</p>
      </relatedmaterial>
    </descgrp>
